WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.649 --> 00:00:08.150
In this video, we'll look at how the player can be linked to the new interactive elements in our level.

2
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:13.650
To start, we're going to take a look at another unique feature to Unreal, the Level Blueprint.

3
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.649
This is a great place for quickly prototyping interactions,

4
00:00:17.649 --> 00:00:24.149
because in the same way that a regular Blueprint has an innate understanding of everything inside of it,

5
00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:29.149
the Level Blueprint has an innate understanding of everything in that level,

6
00:00:29.149 --> 00:00:36.210
which makes it very easy to create references and link different Actors and their components together.

7
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.710
Just be careful you don't rely on this too much,

8
00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.210
as you have to duplicate everything from one Level Blueprint to the next.

9
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:50.479
So if you want to have code that's reusable, you're going to need to have that exist in standalone Actors.

10
00:00:50.479 --> 00:00:55.979
To open the Level Blueprint, we can simply come up here to this Blueprint button,

11
00:00:55.979 --> 00:01:02.479
which gives us a list of everything in the level, and we can see here, open Level Blueprint.

12
00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:06.609
Right now, this is just blank.

13
00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:10.819
We're going to delete both of these,

14
00:01:10.819 --> 00:01:18.819
and we're going to start by once again adding a space bar.

15
00:01:18.819 --> 00:01:25.500
We'll continue to use that for triggering our logic.

16
00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:32.569
If we're wondering how can we now get a reference to our Dancer,

17
00:01:32.569 --> 00:01:38.069
as well as the Stage or the Material Parameter Collection

18
00:01:38.069 --> 00:01:43.069
that's controlling the color of our material for our columns,

19
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:47.069
we're going to be able to do that in a couple ways.

20
00:01:47.069 --> 00:01:50.069
The first, we can make a variable here,

21
00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:56.069
click on the dropdown, and we'll call this Color NPC.

22
00:01:57.819 --> 00:02:01.819
We will change its type to Material.

23
00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:09.129
You can see we get a long list of everything matching what we're typing in,

24
00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:10.629
and this is the one we want.

25
00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:18.360
Material Parameter Collection, hit Compile, and we can set its type to Session Color Change.

26
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.710
In order to get a version of our Dancer,

27
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:27.210
what we need to do is click on them in the level so they're selected,

28
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:34.710
come over here, and now we can right-click and create a reference to our Demo Dancer.

29
00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:40.210
Instead of needing to do a whole searching for an actor of a specific class

30
00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.210
and saving them in a variable,

31
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:48.210
we can just quickly get them right here in our Blueprint.

32
00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:54.909
From this, we want to get our Skeletal Mesh component.

33
00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:58.990
We don't need this, but we do need this.

34
00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.990
We want to get the Animation Instance,

35
00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:05.900
so the Animation Blueprint that is tied to our Skeletal Mesh in that scene.

36
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.990
We'll get an Instance, perfect.

37
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:16.490
And then from here, we want to be able to call that Dance Toggle event

38
00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.990
on that Animation Blueprint.

39
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.990
What we can do is drag off, and we're going to use a Cast node.

40
00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:30.729
This is something kind of unique to C++ and Blueprints.

41
00:03:30.729 --> 00:03:32.729
Session Demo Dancer, there we are.

42
00:03:32.729 --> 00:03:37.729
Casting is maybe a little bit confusing to get your head around at first,

43
00:03:37.729 --> 00:03:43.729
but essentially what this allows us to do is ask if this object that we're inputting,

44
00:03:43.729 --> 00:03:50.729
can we treat this object as this ABP Session Demo Dancer?

45
00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:56.729
If they match, so if this object is this class or a child of it,

46
00:03:56.729 --> 00:04:01.729
then we succeed, and we can execute our logic.

47
00:04:01.729 --> 00:04:06.860
In this case, we can pull off, search for Session Dance Toggle.

48
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.020
There it is, and voila.

49
00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.020
If the cast fails, so it is whatever we get through here,

50
00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.019
it is not an ABP Session Demo Dancer.

51
00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.019
Then this Cast Failed pin will execute,

52
00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:24.720
and we can, for fun, just have a print string saying,

53
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.910
Not Demo Dancer.

54
00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.360
We know something went wrong.

55
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.540
So we want to be able to, before we just pipe this in here,

56
00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:41.540
we also want our space bar to simultaneously be able to change

57
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.730
our color of our material.

58
00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:50.730
So if we want a single, we could, just once this finishes,

59
00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:55.730
be able to drag off from here and start going into maybe another,

60
00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:58.730
our logic that would control that.

61
00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.730
But one thing we can do that's another handy node is a Sequence node.

62
00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:08.730
This allows you to kind of structure your code a little bit more legibly

63
00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.730
and have it going down versus scrolling off to the side in infinity,

64
00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:18.730
and you can add more output pins depending on how many things you want in your sequence,

65
00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.730
and then you can right-click to remove any that you don't need.

66
00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.730
We'll come down to here.

67
00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.850
We'll look for a flip-flop again,

68
00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:33.980
and we want to get our reference to our stage pillar

69
00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.980
or our color material parameter collection.

70
00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.980
We're also going to need a variable for text.

71
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.230
So this is going to be our text.

72
00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.620
This is when we see from here,

73
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.620
when we pull, I'm getting ahead of myself slightly,

74
00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.620
but in order to set the value of that parameter, we have to have its name.

75
00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.970
So if we do set scalar parameter value,

76
00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:04.579
this is what we can use to change it from 0 to 1

77
00:06:04.579 --> 00:06:07.579
and change the color of our material,

78
00:06:07.579 --> 00:06:09.579
but we need to pipe in a name here.

79
00:06:09.579 --> 00:06:18.230
So I guess we'll change this to parameter name.

80
00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:20.230
Always helpful to know what everything is.

81
00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:24.899
We can just send the type of name to grab this.

82
00:06:24.899 --> 00:06:31.540
I kind of like sometimes to situate them up here if I have the room to do that.

83
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.540
You can have your own preferences to how you like to arrange your nodes.

84
00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:41.220
We will set this to, what do we call it?

85
00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.220
Color change A to B.

86
00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.949
Copy that.

87
00:06:44.949 --> 00:06:45.949
Plug that in here.

88
00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:46.949
Fantastic.

89
00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:51.660
And so the first time we're setting it as a default to 0,

90
00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.660
the first time we want to switch it to 1.

91
00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.689
We can duplicate this.

92
00:06:56.689 --> 00:06:59.689
And then once again drag these down.

93
00:06:59.689 --> 00:07:03.519
Set this to 0 the second time.

94
00:07:03.519 --> 00:07:09.920
And now we should be good to go here.

95
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.920
I'm remembering though in our demo dance

96
00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:28.379
that I believe we might have set up our base bar logic.

97
00:07:28.379 --> 00:07:31.689
Where did we do that?

98
00:07:31.689 --> 00:07:32.689
We didn't do that yet.

99
00:07:32.689 --> 00:07:34.689
This had the space bar logic to spin.

100
00:07:34.689 --> 00:07:35.689
That's right.

101
00:07:35.689 --> 00:07:40.689
Okay.

102
00:07:40.689 --> 00:07:43.139
So now we should be good to go here.

103
00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:48.129
If we press play, press space.

104
00:07:48.129 --> 00:07:50.660
Oh, consuming input.

105
00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:53.699
So here's another nice trick to know.

106
00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:56.699
One thing that's important to look out for.

107
00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:06.490
Why didn't our demo dancer here change their dance?

108
00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:09.490
Well, if we open up our spin cube here again

109
00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.490
and look for our space bar,

110
00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.490
we can see that consume input here is checked.

111
00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.490
So this will mean that this instance of this node

112
00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:23.490
on this specific blueprint will use our space bar input.

113
00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.490
And nothing else in our project that uses this space bar input node

114
00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.490
will be able to get that input.

115
00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:34.320
So if we uncheck this, save,

116
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.320
and we come over here and make sure this is also unchecked.

117
00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.700
Save these.

118
00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:44.700
Now, if we play, we should see.

119
00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:45.700
There we go.

120
00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.330
Fantastic stuff.

121
00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:53.330
Both the cube changes spinning direction,

122
00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:57.799
and the dancer changes their dance.

123
00:08:57.799 --> 00:08:59.799
However, we're seeing that we do not have

124
00:08:59.799 --> 00:09:02.799
our super cool material on our boxes yet.

125
00:09:02.799 --> 00:09:05.799
So in order to be able to see our new material

126
00:09:05.799 --> 00:09:07.799
on the pillars around our stage,

127
00:09:07.799 --> 00:09:10.799
we're going to need to be able to see two materials

128
00:09:10.799 --> 00:09:12.799
on that FBX at the same time.

129
00:09:12.799 --> 00:09:14.799
So in order to do that,

130
00:09:14.799 --> 00:09:18.799
we're actually going to reimport this stage object here.

131
00:09:18.799 --> 00:09:21.799
We're going to come up.

132
00:09:21.799 --> 00:09:25.470
I have a version of it here that does not have

133
00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:31.470
the parent selected when we were exporting the FBX out of Unity,

134
00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:34.470
which I'll show, go back and show in a sec.

135
00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:37.470
But we want to make sure we also have this checkbox enabled here,

136
00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:39.470
keep section separate.

137
00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.539
So I'll go ahead and import.

138
00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:43.600
We'll say yes to these.

139
00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.600
And now when we open it up,

140
00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.600
we can see that we have a bunch of new material slots

141
00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.600
for our individual pillars, which is what we want.

142
00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.860
So let's copy this location here.

143
00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:02.940
And actually, we can just copy the whole transform.

144
00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.580
Then we can delete this,

145
00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:14.840
bring in our stage here.

146
00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.320
And it looks like we applied a rotation when we imported it.

147
00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.320
So that looks like it is correct.

148
00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.960
And that's looking good.

149
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.960
So now we have a bunch more materials here.

150
00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.960
So what we can do is drag in our...

151
00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.750
Where was our new material? There it is, stage pillar mat.

152
00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.820
We'll drag that in here.

153
00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.820
There we go.

154
00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:52.860
Fun stuff.

155
00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.600
I'll save.

156
00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:01.970
Now when we press play, look at that.

157
00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:02.970
Now for an activity,

158
00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.970
let's take the logic that we've created in the level blueprint

159
00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.970
and break that out into individual blueprints

160
00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:10.970
for our dancer and our stage

161
00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:12.970
so that we can reuse them in other levels.

162
00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:14.970
In the next video,

163
00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:17.970
we'll look at how we went through this on our end together.
